from kjvlib import text as T
C = [
("h1", "3. Isaiah 28:11, 12 and the Foreign Tongue as a Sign to Israel"),
("p", "Claim: Isaiah 28 is spoken to the rulers of Jerusalem and to this people, and the speech of another tongue in verse 11 is part of a warning to them. Paul quotes the passage in 1 Corinthians 14:21 as written in the law, and the volume reads it first in its own chapter. The passage fixes the audience of the sign before 1 Corinthians 14 is read."),
("sub", "3.1 The chapter and its audience"),
("p", "The chapter opens with a woe: “Woe to the crown of pride, to the drunkards of Ephraim” (Isaiah 28:1). It turns to Judah and Jerusalem, and the reason is stated: “But they also have erred through wine, and through strong drink are out of the way; the priest and the prophet have erred through strong drink” (Isaiah 28:7). The leaders who should teach have failed, and the text goes on to their reply, in which they mock the prophet’s teaching as a lesson for children: “Whom shall he teach knowledge? and whom shall he make to understand doctrine? them that are weaned from the milk, and drawn from the breasts” (Isaiah 28:9)."),
("p", "The prophet answers in the next verses: “For with stammering lips and another tongue will he speak to this people” (Isaiah 28:11). The speaker is the LORD, the hearers are “this people,” and the speech is in a tongue that is not theirs. The next verse reports what had been said to them and what they did: “To whom he said, This is the rest wherewith ye may cause the weary to rest; and this is the refreshing: yet they would not hear” (Isaiah 28:12). The people were offered rest, and they would not hear."),
("p", "The passage closes the thought with the consequence. “But the word of the LORD was unto them precept upon precept, precept upon precept; line upon line, line upon line; here a little, and there a little; that they might go, and fall backward, and be broken, and snared, and taken” (Isaiah 28:13). The prophet then names the audience again: “Wherefore hear the word of the LORD, ye scornful men, that rule this people which is in Jerusalem” (Isaiah 28:14). The people in verse 11 are the people ruled from Jerusalem, and the scornful men are their rulers."),
("tbl", "Isaiah 28:7–14 in order.", [
    ("Isaiah 28:7", T("Isa 28:7"), "The priest and the prophet of Israel have erred, and the teachers of the people are named."),
    ("Isaiah 28:11, 12", T("Isa 28:11, 12"), "The LORD speaks to this people with stammering lips and another tongue; they were offered rest and would not hear."),
    ("Isaiah 28:13", T("Isa 28:13"), "The word given in small pieces becomes the occasion of their falling."),
    ("Isaiah 28:14", T("Isa 28:14"), "The audience is stated: the scornful men who rule this people in Jerusalem."),
  ], [0.2, 0.5, 0.3]),
("sub", "3.2 The foreign tongue as judgment in Israel’s own law"),
("p", "The prophet’s sentence has a precedent in the law. When Moses sets out the curses for disobedience, he says, “The LORD shall bring a nation against thee from far, from the end of the earth, as swift as the eagle flieth; a nation whose tongue thou shalt not understand” (Deuteronomy 28:49). Jeremiah repeats the warning to the house of Israel: “Lo, I will bring a nation upon you from far, O house of Israel, saith the LORD: it is a mighty nation, it is an ancient nation, a nation whose language thou knowest not, neither understandest what they say” (Jeremiah 5:15). In both passages a language the people cannot understand comes to them as the sign of the LORD’s judgment on Israel."),
("p", "Isaiah 28:11 stands in that line. A foreign tongue spoken to Israel is the mark that the LORD is speaking in a way the people can no longer take as their own. The volume does not say that the Corinthian gift is the same event as Assyria’s speech or Babylon’s. It says that Isaiah, Moses and Jeremiah give the foreign tongue a meaning in Israel’s scriptures, and that Paul quotes from within that body of scripture."),
("sub", "3.3 Paul’s wording"),
("p", "Paul does not quote Isaiah word for word as the KJV prints the prophet. He writes, “In the law it is written, With men of other tongues and other lips will I speak unto this people; and yet for all that will they not hear me, saith the Lord” (1 Corinthians 14:21). The words “other tongues” and “other lips” reverse the order of Isaiah’s “stammering lips and another tongue,” and “yet for all that will they not hear me” is the sense of Isaiah’s “yet they would not hear.” The volume reports the difference and holds that the quotation is recognizable: it keeps the speaker, the people, the foreign speech and the refusal to hear."),
("p", "Paul calls the passage “the law.” The word is used in the New Testament for the whole of Israel’s scripture where a prophet is cited, and the volume reads Paul as naming the scripture of Israel as the place where the sign is written. He adds nothing about a new audience. “This people” in his quotation is the people of Isaiah’s sentence."),
("sub", "3.4 Babel and the Pentecost scene"),
("p", "A third place bears on the sense. At Babel “the whole earth was of one language, and of one speech” (Genesis 11:1), and the LORD said, “let us go down, and there confound their language, that they may not understand one another’s speech” (Genesis 11:7). The scene at Babel is judgment by languages. The scene at Pentecost, in which the multitude “were confounded, because that every man heard them speak in his own language” (Acts 2:6), reverses the effect: the languages are heard and understood. The volume reports the correspondence and draws from it only that the book of Acts places the languages of Pentecost among devout Jews from every nation, and that the sign of tongues first appears in the book to Israel."),
("sub", "3.5 The rest of the chapter"),
("p", "The chapter does not end with the tongue. The rulers answer the prophet with a boast: “Because ye have said, We have made a covenant with death, and with hell are we at agreement; when the overflowing scourge shall pass through, it shall not come unto us: for we have made lies our refuge, and under falsehood have we hid ourselves” (Isaiah 28:15). The LORD replies at once: “Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD, Behold, I lay in Zion for a foundation a stone, a tried stone, a precious corner stone, a sure foundation: he that believeth shall not make haste” (Isaiah 28:16). The chapter sets the unbelief of Jerusalem’s rulers beside the stone that the LORD lays, and the rulers are told, “be ye not mockers, lest your bands be made strong” (Isaiah 28:22)."),
("p", "The tongue of verse 11 is therefore part of a charge against the unbelieving leaders of Israel. It is a judgment-sign in the setting of a promise: the stone in Zion is still laid, and he that believeth is addressed. The volume reads Paul’s “them that believe not” with that setting. The sign speaks to an unbelief that the prophet has described in detail, and it is given to a people who have been offered rest and have refused it."),
("ctbl", "Where the foreign tongue appears in Israel’s scripture.", [
    ("Passage", "Who speaks, to whom", "What it states"),
    ("Genesis 11:1, 7, 9", "The LORD, to the earth", "The languages confounded at Babel"),
    ("Deuteronomy 28:49", "Moses, to Israel", "A nation whose tongue they shall not understand, as a curse"),
    ("Isaiah 28:11, 12", "The LORD, to this people", "Another tongue spoken; they would not hear"),
    ("Jeremiah 5:15", "The LORD, to the house of Israel", "A nation whose language they know not"),
    ("1 Corinthians 14:21", "The Lord, to this people, as Paul quotes", "Other tongues and other lips; they will not hear"),
  ], [0.25, 0.30, 0.45]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "Isaiah 28:11, 12 is spoken by the LORD to this people, the people ruled from Jerusalem, and it reports that they would not hear.",
    "The foreign tongue is a mark of the LORD’s dealing with Israel in the law and the prophets.",
    "Paul’s quotation in 1 Corinthians 14:21 differs in the order of the words and keeps the speaker, the audience and the refusal.",
    "Paul calls the passage the law and names no other audience for the sign.",
  ]),
]
